{"archetypes":[{"name":"cross-doc-consistency","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Doc-only change spanning two tightly-coupled reference files; cross-reference fidelity is the primary risk.","prompt_body":"Examine every cross-reference between conflict-resolution.md and rebase-rebump-subprocedure.md in the diff: caller_kind token names, flag strings (--no-push, --keep-on-conflict, --continue), Phase numbering, exit-code tables, bail destinations (STALL_TRACKING=true → Step 18 vs bail to 12d), and MANDATORY-READ-ENTIRE-FILE directives. Verify that each side's description of the other is mutually consistent — e.g., that the sub-procedure's step-2 exit-1 branch for step8b_rebase matches exactly what conflict-resolution.md Phase 4 says it dispatches, and vice versa. Flag any asymmetry where one file describes behavior that the other file does not corroborate or contradicts. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"caller-kind-contract","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"caller_kind is a load-bearing contract token; any mismatch in how it is threaded through Phase 4 re-entry and sub-procedure step 7 return is a silent logic error.","prompt_body":"Trace the full lifecycle of caller_kind=step8b_rebase through the diff: from the sub-procedure step-2 conflict dispatch, through Phase 1–4 in conflict-resolution.md, through Phase 4 exit-0 re-entry into the sub-procedure with rebase_already_done=true, through steps 3–7 of the sub-procedure, to the step-7 return to implement-finalize.sh postbump. Check that each handoff uses the exact same token value, that rebase_already_done=true correctly skips steps 1–2 on re-entry, and that step 5 (push) is explicitly skipped for step8b_rebase at the Phase-4-caller-path section. Also confirm the step-7 return path for step8b_rebase is not accidentally shared with or overwritten by the step12_phase4 return path. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bail-path-completeness","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"Bail paths are numerous and differ between caller families; a missing git-rebase-abort.sh call or wrong destination on any bail leg would leave the repo in a broken state.","prompt_body":"Enumerate every bail exit path introduced or modified for caller_kind=step8b_rebase in both files: Phase 1 bail, Phase 2 user-abort bail, Phase 3 skip confirmation, Phase 4 exit-1 loop, Phase 4 exit-3 error, and Phase 1–4 bail from the sub-procedure step-2 dispatch. For each, verify: (1) git-rebase-abort.sh is called before the bail destination when a rebase is in progress, (2) STALL_TRACKING=true is set, (3) the destination is Step 18 (not Step 12d), and (4) no conflicting instruction says to bail to Step 12d instead. Cross-check the Bail invariant section of conflict-resolution.md against each bail leg. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
